<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsar was the title of the supreme ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Caesar, formally adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in 1547. It expressed claims to imperial status and to succession from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> later added the title of Emperor, but tsar remained in common use until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1917, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> fought several wars against the tsars of Muscovy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsar.jpg" alt="Tsar" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse Mucha · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Car_Simeon_Bulharsky_-_Alfons_Mucha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tsar, also spelled czar, is a Slavic title derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> caesar. It entered the Slavic languages through the Gothic kaisar and served as the equivalent of the Greek basileus, which referred to Byzantine emperors and the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. It was meant to denote an emperor in the medieval European sense, a ruler of the same rank as a Roman emperor who held it by the approval of another emperor or a supreme ecclesiastical official, but Western Europeans usually took it to mean king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first ruler to adopt the title was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a>, after a makeshift imperial coronation performed by the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 913. The Byzantine government recognised it in 924 and again in 927, after a decade of intensive warfare. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was crowned emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 1346, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> succeeded him in 1355. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s Ferdinand I took the title again in 1908, and it lasted until the monarchy ended in 1946.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, church officials once called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> tsar, but other princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> did not style themselves so. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>, who asserted independence from the khan in 1476, began using the title regularly in diplomacy with the West. Its growth was tied to Russian ambitions to be a third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, and Emperor Maximilian I recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> monarch as an emperor in 1514. The first Russian ruler formally crowned tsar of all Russia was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in 1547.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1721 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> formally adopted the title of Emperor, but tsar stayed in common use and was kept in the full title as the style of rule over states absorbed by the Russian monarchy, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> khanates and the Georgian Orthodox kingdom. In the 18th century it was increasingly seen as inferior to emperor.</p><p>By 1815 it had come to be read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as the equivalent of the Polish król, king, and the emperor took the title tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Among the indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, Russian autocracy became identified with the White Tsar. In English, tsar has been used since 1866 as a metaphor for a person of high authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his defeat in Russia in 1812, Prussia insisted that the duchy be eliminated, while <b>Tsar</b> Alexander I wished to annex it; Austria and the United Kingdom, through Lord Castlereagh, strongly opposed him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 10th century, under <b>Tsar</b> Peter I, the priest Bogomil founded a dualist religious movement, the Bogomils, which rejected the church hierarchy and the material world and later spread to Byzantium and western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tsar</b> Simeon I of Bulgaria employed them against the Magyars, and after the Battle of Southern Buh they drove the Magyars out of Etelköz and the Pontic steppes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1610, after the victory at Klushino, Polish troops occupied Moscow and a group of boyars elected the Polish prince Władysław as <b>Tsar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan IV ('the Terrible'), who reigned from 1547 to 1584, was crowned <b>Tsar</b> and began the eastward expansion beyond the Ural Mountains into Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol attacks from 1242 forced the tsars to pay tribute, and in 1277 the swineherd Ivailo led a peasant rising that beat off Mongol raiders and killed <b>Tsar</b> Constantine Tikh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
